<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#geez">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ge'ez</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ge'ez script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ge'ez is an ancient Semitic language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, with its own script. It was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>'s inscriptions and became the liturgical and literary language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and its church, flourishing under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geez.jpg" alt="Ge'ez" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aksum,_iscrizione_di_re_ezana,_in_greco,_sabeo_e_ge%27ez,_330-350_dc_ca._11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
